   "key": "jsut",
   "title": "JSUT",
   "subtitle": "Monolingual Japanese read speech",
   "desc": "A sanity check that our code-switching fine-tuning does not degrade clean monolingual speech. On standard Japanese read speech, PhARO, its base model and the human ground-truth recordings all reach similarly low pronunciation error (PFER, the same measure used in the other datasets), while PhARO stays ahead of the commercial systems — confirming the RL objective preserves monolingual quality rather than trading it away. English-voiced commercial systems still tend to anglicize embedded loanwords (e.g. ElevenLabs renders “エンパイアステートビル” closer to English “Empire State”).",
